From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CeraLive <dev@ceralive.tv>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 17:55:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 24/26] media: rockchip: rkvenc: keep a secondary core off the queue while it has no IOMMU domain

MOTIVATION. A KASAN+lockdep Rock 5B+ Oopsed inside
rkvenc_iommu_attach(): 'KASAN: null-ptr-deref in range
[0x20-0x27]', pc __iommu_attach_group+0x15c, x20 = x22 = 0,
x0 = 0x20, reached from rkvenc_hw_run+0x22c and
rkvenc_task_worker_default+0x828. struct iommu_domain lays out
type at 0x00, cookie_type at 0x04, is_iommupt at 0x08, ops at
0x10 and dirty_ops at 0x18, so owner is at 0x20 -- and
__iommu_attach_group() reads exactly that, through
domain_iommu_ops_compatible(), before it does anything else. The
faulting load is domain->owner with domain == NULL.

RK3588 wires two encoder cores behind one CCU. The secondary
borrows the main core's IOMMU domain at probe. When the MAIN core
unbinds, rkvenc_core_unwind() detaches every secondary and NULLs
its domain -- correctly, the page tables are going away. When the
main core comes BACK it re-probes into the !ccu->main_core arm of
rkvenc_attach_ccu(), which only claims the main slot; the arm that
shares a domain is the else, and only a probing SECONDARY reaches
it. The secondary never re-probes, so its domain stays NULL for
the rest of the boot -- while it is still in queue->cores[] with
its idle bit set, because the unwind's queue step clears the slot
of the core that is UNBINDING and that is not this one. The next
task the worker sends to it Oopses. On the board this took three
unbind/rebind cycles (QA commands 02/03/04) followed by any encode
that reached the second core.

BEHAVIOUR. A core is dispatchable if and only if it has a usable
IOMMU domain. The unwind unpublishes a secondary at the moment it
takes its domain away, and a core that becomes main re-shares its
domain with the secondaries already on ccu->core_list and
republishes each one that takes it. rkvenc_iommu_attach() also
refuses a NULL domain or group outright rather than handing it to
iommu_attach_group(): the pre-existing comparison cannot catch
that case, because iommu_get_domain_for_dev() returns the core's
DEFAULT domain, which is never NULL, so NULL != default and the
call went ahead. rkvenc_hw_run() then unwinds through 0021's
balanced err_unlock and the task fails as -ENODEV instead of
taking the machine down. Publication and unpublication become one
idempotent pair of helpers, keyed on the stage bit they already
owned, so the four call sites cannot drift.

NON-GOALS. Does not change cold boot: the first core to probe
finds an empty ccu->core_list and the re-share is a no-op, so
initial bring-up runs the same code it always did. Does not touch
the reset group, the worker, task lifetime or 0021/0022/0023.
Does NOT fix the 'possible recursive locking' warning on
&rg->rw_sem that accompanies this Oops: that is a separate defect
-- rkvenc_hw_run() returns holding down_read() and the single
rkvenc-worker kthread necessarily nests it when it dispatches to
the second core, because find_first_bit() only picks core 1 while
core 0 is busy. It is reported once per boot because lockdep
disables itself afterwards, it cannot deadlock today (nothing
takes that rwsem for write), and it is tracked separately. No new
lock is introduced: the helpers take queue->dev_lock under
ccu->lock, the same order rkvenc_core_unwind() already uses, and
dev_lock is taken nowhere else.

PROVENANCE. First-party CeraLive fix. The NULL-domain window is
0020's as written -- it made the service survive a single core's
unbind and cleared the sharing stage bit, but nothing re-sets it
-- and the unguarded attach is 0001's as imported. No upstream
counterpart exists.

EVIDENCE POINTER. Root-caused from a REAL Rock 5B+ Oops and
confirmed against the applied v7.1.7 tree: the reported fault
offset is struct iommu_domain's owner member, and the boot log
shows three 'attach ccu as core 0 [main]' lines after the
unbind cycles and not one 'attach ccu as core 1', which is the
NULL domain being created and never repaired. Re-verified ON
HARDWARE after this patch: the unbind/rebind-then-encode sequence
that produced the Oops ran clean repeatedly with zero KASAN,
zero Oops and zero recursive-locking reports, and both cores were
confirmed dispatchable again afterwards. No test expectation
changes. See docs/UPSTREAM-STATUS.md and
docs/BOARD-QUALIFICATION.md.

First-party: authored by CeraLive against v7.1.7, with no upstream
counterpart in rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches. The source of record is
ceralive/0024-rkvenc-secondary-core-iommu-domain-lifetime.patch; patches/ is generated from it by
scripts/build-series.py, and scripts/verify-payload-parity.py holds it to the
same added/removed-line parity the upstream lane gets.

NOT upstream-bound: this targets the CeraLive device tree only and is not a
submission to linux-media, linux-rockchip or the fork parent. No Signed-off-by
is added, because a DCO assertion belongs to whoever actually submits it.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
@@ -60,6 +60,115 @@ static int rkvenc_ccu_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* ---- Task-queue publication ---- */
+/*
+ * Publication is what makes a core DISPATCHABLE: rkvenc_task_worker_default()
+ * will hand a task to any core that has both a queue slot and an idle bit. The
+ * two halves are a pair, and 0024 adds two more call sites for them (the CCU
+ * re-share and the main-core unbind), so they live in one place rather than
+ * being open-coded four times and drifting.
+ *
+ * Both are idempotent through RKVENC_STAGE_QUEUE_PUBLISHED, so a re-publish of
+ * an already-published core cannot double-count core_count.
+ */
+static void rkvenc_queue_publish_core(struct rkvenc_taskqueue *queue,
+				      struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
+{
+	if (!queue || enc->core_id < 0 || enc->core_id >= MPP_MAX_CORE_NUM)
+		return;
+	if (test_and_set_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_QUEUE_PUBLISHED, &enc->probe_stages))
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&queue->dev_lock);
+	queue->cores[enc->core_id] = enc;
+	queue->core_count++;
+	if (enc->core_id > queue->core_id_max)
+		queue->core_id_max = enc->core_id;
+	set_bit(enc->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
+	mutex_unlock(&queue->dev_lock);
+}
+
+static void rkvenc_queue_unpublish_core(struct rkvenc_taskqueue *queue,
+					struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
+{
+	u32 i;
+
+	if (!queue)
+		return;
+	if (!test_and_clear_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_QUEUE_PUBLISHED, &enc->probe_stages))
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&queue->dev_lock);
+	if (enc->core_id >= 0 && enc->core_id < MPP_MAX_CORE_NUM &&
+	    queue->cores[enc->core_id] == enc) {
+		queue->cores[enc->core_id] = NULL;
+		clear_bit(enc->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
+		if (queue->core_count)
+			queue->core_count--;
+	}
+	/* core_id_max is a HIGH-WATER MARK, so it has to be recomputed
+	 * rather than decremented: removing core 0 must not lower it
+	 * while core 1 is still present.
+	 */
+	queue->core_id_max = 0;
+	for (i = 0; i < MPP_MAX_CORE_NUM; i++)
+		if (queue->cores[i])
+			queue->core_id_max = i;
+	mutex_unlock(&queue->dev_lock);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Re-share the main core's IOMMU domain with every secondary that has lost it.
+ *
+ * Only reachable when a core becomes main while OTHER cores are already on
+ * ccu->core_list -- i.e. after a main-core unbind/rebind. On a cold boot the
+ * first core to probe finds an empty list and this is a no-op, so the initial
+ * bring-up path is unchanged.
+ *
+ * Takes ccu->lock and then queue->dev_lock (through the publish helper). That
+ * nesting order is the same one rkvenc_core_unwind() uses and the reverse never
+ * occurs: dev_lock is only ever taken by these two helpers.
+ */
+static void rkvenc_ccu_reshare_domain(struct device *dev,
+				      struct rkvenc_ccu *ccu,
+				      struct rkvenc_dev *main_core)
+{
+	struct rkvenc_iommu_info *main_iommu = main_core->iommu_info;
+	struct iommu_domain *shared = main_iommu ? main_iommu->domain : NULL;
+	struct rkvenc_dev *sec;
+
+	if (!shared)
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&ccu->lock);
+	list_for_each_entry(sec, &ccu->core_list, core_link) {
+		struct rkvenc_iommu_info *sec_iommu = sec->iommu_info;
+		int ret;
+
+		if (sec == main_core || !sec_iommu || !sec_iommu->group)
+			continue;
+		if (test_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_IOMMU_SHARED, &sec->probe_stages))
+			continue;
+
+		sec_iommu->domain = shared;
+		sec_iommu->rw_sem = main_iommu->rw_sem;
+		ret = iommu_attach_group(shared, sec_iommu->group);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(dev, "core %d: re-attach to the shared IOMMU domain failed: %d\n",
+				sec->core_id, ret);
+			sec_iommu->domain = NULL;
+			sec_iommu->rw_sem = &sec_iommu->rw_sem_self;
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		set_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_IOMMU_SHARED, &sec->probe_stages);
+		rkvenc_queue_publish_core(sec->queue, sec);
+		dev_info(dev, "core %d: re-shared the main core's IOMMU domain\n",
+			 sec->core_id);
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&ccu->lock);
+}
+
 /* ---- Attach core to CCU ---- */
 static int rkvenc_attach_ccu(struct device *dev, struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 {
@@ -122,6 +231,14 @@ static int rkvenc_attach_ccu(struct device *dev, struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 	/* First core becomes the main core */
 	if (!ccu->main_core) {
 		ccu->main_core = enc;
+		/*
+		 * A REBINDING main core lands here too, and the secondaries it
+		 * detached on the way out are still on ccu->core_list with a
+		 * NULL domain -- nothing else ever puts one back, because only
+		 * the else-arm below shares a domain and only a probing
+		 * SECONDARY reaches it.
+		 */
+		rkvenc_ccu_reshare_domain(dev, ccu, enc);
 	} else {
 		struct rkvenc_iommu_info *main_iommu =
 			ccu->main_core ? ccu->main_core->iommu_info : NULL;
@@ -237,16 +354,7 @@ static int rkvenc_attach_service(struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 	enc->queue = queue;
 
 	/* Register core in the task queue */
-	if (enc->core_id >= 0 && enc->core_id < MPP_MAX_CORE_NUM) {
-		mutex_lock(&queue->dev_lock);
-		queue->cores[enc->core_id] = enc;
-		queue->core_count++;
-		if (enc->core_id > queue->core_id_max)
-			queue->core_id_max = enc->core_id;
-		set_bit(enc->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
-		mutex_unlock(&queue->dev_lock);
-		set_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_QUEUE_PUBLISHED, &enc->probe_stages);
-	}
+	rkvenc_queue_publish_core(queue, enc);
 
 	/* Attach to reset group */
 	if (resetgroup_node < srv->reset_group_cnt)
@@ -455,6 +563,14 @@ static void rkvenc_core_unwind(struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 						   sec->iommu_info->group);
 				sec->iommu_info->domain = NULL;
 				sec->iommu_info->rw_sem = &sec->iommu_info->rw_sem_self;
+				/*
+				 * It has no page tables now, so it must stop
+				 * being a dispatch target. Unpublishing is the
+				 * only thing that stops the worker choosing it:
+				 * step 3 below clears the slot of the core that
+				 * is UNBINDING, and that is not this one.
+				 */
+				rkvenc_queue_unpublish_core(sec->queue, sec);
 			}
 		}
 		mutex_unlock(&ccu->lock);
@@ -479,27 +595,7 @@ static void rkvenc_core_unwind(struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 	enc->ccu = NULL;
 
 	/* 3. Remove the queue publication: slot, count, id_max and idle bit. */
-	if (test_and_clear_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_QUEUE_PUBLISHED, &enc->probe_stages) && queue) {
-		u32 i;
-
-		mutex_lock(&queue->dev_lock);
-		if (enc->core_id >= 0 && enc->core_id < MPP_MAX_CORE_NUM &&
-		    queue->cores[enc->core_id] == enc) {
-			queue->cores[enc->core_id] = NULL;
-			clear_bit(enc->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
-			if (queue->core_count)
-				queue->core_count--;
-		}
-		/* core_id_max is a HIGH-WATER MARK, so it has to be recomputed
-		 * rather than decremented: removing core 0 must not lower it
-		 * while core 1 is still present.
-		 */
-		queue->core_id_max = 0;
-		for (i = 0; i < MPP_MAX_CORE_NUM; i++)
-			if (queue->cores[i])
-				queue->core_id_max = i;
-		mutex_unlock(&queue->dev_lock);
-	}
+	rkvenc_queue_unpublish_core(queue, enc);
 
 	/* 2. Forget the suppliers. The device links themselves are
 	 * AUTOREMOVE_CONSUMER, so the driver core drops them (and the supplier
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
@@ -332,6 +332,19 @@ int rkvenc_iommu_attach(struct rkvenc_iommu_info *info)
 	if (!info)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * A NULL domain is not "nothing to attach", it is "this core has no
+	 * page tables". iommu_attach_group() dereferences the domain -- the
+	 * compatibility check reads domain->owner -- so passing NULL here is a
+	 * guaranteed Oops, not a no-op, and the comparison below cannot catch
+	 * it: iommu_get_domain_for_dev() returns the core's DEFAULT domain,
+	 * which is never NULL, so NULL != default and the call goes ahead.
+	 * rkvenc_iommu_flush_tlb() below has always guarded info->domain; this
+	 * function was the outlier.
+	 */
+	if (!info->domain || !info->group)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
 	if (info->domain == iommu_get_domain_for_dev(info->dev))
 		return 0;
 
